{"id":"configuration-secret","corpus":"k8s","title":"Secrets","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Secrets"},{"kind":"p","text":"A Secret is an object that contains a small amount of sensitive data such as a password, a token, or a key. Such information might otherwise be put in a Pod specification or in a container image. Using a Secret means that you don't need to include confidential data in your application code."},{"kind":"p","text":"Because Secrets can be created independently of the Pods that use them, there is less risk of the Secret (and its data) being exposed during the workflow of creating, viewing, and editing Pods. Kubernetes, and applications that run in your cluster, can also take additional precautions with Secrets, such as avoiding writing sensitive data to nonvolatile storage."},{"kind":"p","text":"Secrets are similar to ConfigMaps but are specifically intended to hold confidential data."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes Secrets are, by default, stored unencrypted in the API server's underlying data store (etcd). Anyone with API access can retrieve or modify a Secret, and so can anyone with access to etcd. Additionally, anyone who is authorized to create a Pod in a namespace can use that access to read any Secret in that namespace; this includes indirect access such as the ability to create a Deployment."},{"kind":"p","text":"In order to safely use Secrets, take at least the following steps:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Enable Encryption at Rest for Secrets."},{"kind":"li","text":"Enable or configure RBAC rules with least-privilege access to Secrets."},{"kind":"li","text":"Restrict Secret access to specific containers."},{"kind":"li","text":"Consider using external Secret store providers."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more guidelines to manage and improve the security of your Secrets, refer to Good practices for Kubernetes Secrets."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Information security for Secrets for more details."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Uses for Secrets"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can use Secrets for purposes such as the following:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Set environment variables for a container."},{"kind":"li","text":"Provide credentials such as SSH keys or passwords to Pods."},{"kind":"li","text":"Allow the kubelet to pull container images from private registries."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Kubernetes control plane also uses Secrets; for example, bootstrap token Secrets are a mechanism to help automate node registration."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Use case: dotfiles in a secret volume"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can make your data \"hidden\" by defining a key that begins with a dot. This key represents a dotfile or \"hidden\" file. For example, when the following Secret is mounted into a volume, secret-volume, the volume will contain a single file, called .secret-file, and the dotfile-test-container will have this file present at the path /etc/secret-volume/.secret-file."},{"kind":"p","text":"Files beginning with dot characters are hidden from the output of ls -l; you must use ls -la to see them when listing directory contents."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Use case: Secret visible to one container in a Pod"},{"kind":"p","text":"Consider a program that needs to handle HTTP requests, do some complex business logic, and then sign some messages with an HMAC. Because it has complex application logic, there might be an unnoticed remote file reading exploit in the server, which could expose the private key to an attacker."},{"kind":"p","text":"This could be divided into two processes in two containers: a frontend container which handles user interaction and business logic, but which cannot see the private key; and a signer container that can see the private key, and responds to simple signing requests from the frontend (for example, over localhost networking)."},{"kind":"p","text":"With this partitioned approach, an attacker now has to trick the application server into doing something rather arbitrary, which may be harder than getting it to read a file."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Alternatives to Secrets"},{"kind":"p","text":"Rather than using a Secret to protect confidential data, you can pick from alternatives."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here are some of your options:"},{"kind":"li","text":"If your cloud-native component needs to authenticate to another application that you know is running within the same Kubernetes cluster, you can use a ServiceAccount and its tokens to identify your client."},{"kind":"li","text":"There are third-party tools that you can run, either within or outside your cluster, that manage sensitive data. For example, a service that Pods access over HTTPS, that reveals a Secret if the client correctly authenticates (for example, with a ServiceAccount token)."},{"kind":"li","text":"For authentication, you can implement a custom signer for X.509 certificates, and use CertificateSigningRequests to let that custom signer issue certificates to Pods that need them."},{"kind":"li","text":"You can use a device plugin to expose node-local encryption hardware to a specific Pod. For example, you can schedule trusted Pods onto nodes that provide a Trusted Platform Module, configured out-of-band."}],"lemmas":{"secrets":["secret","secrets"],"objects":["object","objects"],"objected":["objected","object"],"objecting":["objecting","object"],"contains":["contains","contain"],"containing":["containing","contain"],"contained":["contained","contain"],"smalls":["small","smalls"],"smaller":["smaller","small"],"amounts":["amount","amounts"],"amounted":["amounted","amount"],"amounting":["amounting","amount"],"sensitives":["sensitive","sensitives"],"data":["datum","data"],"keying":["keying","key"],"keyed":["keyed","key"],"otherwises":["otherwises","otherwise"],"puts":["put","puts"],"putting":["putting","put"],"spec